      <sec>
        <title>Assembly quality metrics</title>
        <p>The BUSCO completeness scores, estimated Quality Value (QV) and
          k-mer completeness metrics were computed for each haplotype and the
          combined assembly. BUSCO scores provide a measure of completeness
          based on benchmarking universal single-copy orthologs. The QV
          measures the base-level consensus accuracy of the genome assembly
          while completeness indicates the proportion of expected k-mers
          identified in the assembly.</p>
        <p>BUSCO analysis performed using the {{ busco_lineage }} reference
          database identified {{ hap1_busco_complete_pct }}% of expected genes
          in haplotype 1 (single copy {{ hap1_busco_single }}%, duplicated
          {{ hap1_busco_dup }}%) and {{ hap2_busco_complete_pct }}% in
          haplotype 2 (single copy {{ hap2_busco_single }}%, duplicated
          {{ hap2_busco_dup }}%).</p>
        <p>The QV for haplotype 1 is {{ hap1_qv }}, and {{ hap2_qv }} for
          haplotype 2. Combining both haplotypes yields a QV of
          {{ combined_qv }}. The k-mer completeness for haplotype 1 is
          {{ hap1_kmer_completeness }}% and {{ hap2_kmer_completeness }}% for
          haplotype 2. The combined completeness of both haplotypes is
          {{ combined_kmer_completeness }}%.
          <xref ref-type="fig" rid="f4">Figure 4</xref> shows the frequency
          distribution of k-mers that are unique to each haplotype (red and
          blue curves) and shared between the haplotypes (green curve).</p>
        <p>The snail plot in
          <xref ref-type="fig" rid="f5">Figure 5</xref> provides a
          scaffold-level overview of the assembly. Scaffolds are arranged by
          length around the circumference, with arcs highlighting the largest
          scaffold and the N50 and N90 lengths. The plot also displays GC, AT,
          and N content across scaffold bins, as well as a cumulative count of
          scaffolds in the centre. The blob plot in
          <xref ref-type="fig" rid="f6">Figure 6</xref> visualises GC content
          on the horizontal axis and coverage on the vertical axis, with each
          point representing a scaffold sized by length.</p>

        <!-- Figure 4: MerquryFK k-mer plot -->
        <fig fig-type="figure" id="f4" orientation="portrait" position="float">
          <label>Figure 4. </label>
          <caption>
            <title>Evaluation of k-mer completeness using MerquryFK for
              <italic>{{ species_name }}</italic> {{ hap1_assembly_name }}.</title>
            <p>This plot illustrates the recovery of k-mers from the original
              read data in the final assemblies. The horizontal axis shows
              k-mer multiplicity, and the vertical axis shows the number of
              k-mers. The grey curve represents k-mers that appear in the reads
              but are not assembled. The green curve corresponds to k-mers
              shared by both assemblies (the homozygous peak), and the red and
              blue curves show k-mers found only in haplotype 1 or haplotype 2,
              respectively (the heterozygous peaks).</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic orientation="portrait" position="float"
                   xlink:href="{{ merqury_kmer_plot_path }}"/>
        </fig>

        <!-- Figure 5: Snail plot -->
        <fig fig-type="figure" id="f5" orientation="portrait" position="float">
          <label>Figure 5. </label>
          <caption>
            <title>Genome assembly of
              <italic>{{ species_name }}</italic> {{ hap1_assembly_name }}: metrics.</title>
            <p>The BlobToolKit snail plot provides an overview of assembly
              metrics and BUSCO gene completeness. The circumference represents
              the length of the whole genome sequence, and the main plot is
              divided into 1,000 bins around the circumference. The outermost
              blue tracks display the distribution of GC, AT, and N percentages
              across the bins. Scaffolds are arranged clockwise from longest to
              shortest and are depicted in dark grey. The longest scaffold is
              indicated by the red arc, and the deeper orange and pale orange
              arcs represent the N50 and N90 lengths. A light grey spiral at
              the centre shows the cumulative scaffold count on a logarithmic
              scale. A summary of complete, fragmented, duplicated, and missing
              BUSCO genes in the set is presented at the top right.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic orientation="portrait" position="float"
                   xlink:href="{{ hap1_snail_plot_path }}"/>
        </fig>

        <!-- Figure 6: Blob/GC-coverage plot -->
        <fig fig-type="figure" id="f6" orientation="portrait" position="float">
          <label>Figure 6. </label>
          <caption>
            <title>Genome assembly of
              <italic>{{ species_name }}</italic> {{ hap1_assembly_name }}: BlobToolKit GC-coverage plot.</title>
            <p>Blob plot showing sequence coverage (vertical axis) and GC
              content (horizontal axis). The circles represent scaffolds, with
              the size proportional to scaffold length and the colour
              representing phylum membership. The histograms along the axes
              display the total length of sequences distributed across different
              levels of coverage and GC content.</p>
          </caption>
          <graphic orientation="portrait" position="float"
                   xlink:href="{{ hap1_blob_plot_path }}"/>
        </fig>

      </sec>
    </sec>

    <!-- ── Data use and attribution ── -->
    <sec>
      <title>Data use and attribution</title>
      <p>OceanOmics Centre is committed to providing data and capabilities for
        public benefit that are actively communicated to and used by the
        scientific, conservation management and wider communities. All users of
        OceanOmics Centre data and capabilities are expected to demonstrate
        professional courtesy and scientific integrity, and to use the data in
        ways that advance Minderoo&#x2019;s charitable purposes of protecting
        and enhancing the natural environment, whilst promoting scientific
        research and the provision of information education relating to the
        natural environment.</p>
      <p>Users of these data agree to do so in accordance with our Data Use
        and Sharing Policy and are expected to cite this genome note and its
        DOI. Users should further cite the source of the data and support by
        including the following standard attribution statement in the
        acknowledgement section of research outputs:
        &#x201c;We acknowledge funding and support of Minderoo Foundation and
        Minderoo OceanOmics Centre at UWA in this work and its mission to
        protect and restore natural ecosystems for future
        generations&#x201d;.</p>
      <p>Notification of communications and research outputs that make use of
        OceanOmics Centre data and capabilities are requested to be sent to
        <email xlink:href="mailto:oceanomicscentre@uwa.edu.au">oceanomicscentre@uwa.edu.au</email>
        to assist with our impact measurement and learning. We welcome
        collaborators and data users to share impact stories that describe how
        the data or support provided by OceanOmics Centre has elevated their
        work.</p>
    </sec>

